// ============================================================
// Berg`s — Code panel
// All Perchance plugin imports + metadata live here.
// The engine, UI, CSS, and HTML all live inline in the panel aside ->
// block; the engine is inlined now.
// ============================================================


// --- Plugin imports (required) ---
ai = {import:ai-text-plugin}                          // long-form generation
image = {import:text-to-image-plugin}                 // scene image generator
commentsPlugin = ""                                   // removed       
tabbedCommentsPlugin = ""                             // removed
fullscreenButton = {import:fullscreen-button-plugin}  // fullscreen control
literal = {import:literal-plugin}                     // safely embed user text with () / {}
upload = {import:upload-plugin}                       // share-link uploads
kv = {import:kv-plugin}                               // share-link key-value store
prompt2 = {import:prompt2-plugin}                     // modal prompts (used by shortcut editor)
bugReport = {import:bug-report-plugin}                // debug capture for feedback comments
diag = {import:berg-auto-diagnostic-system}           // 🔬 diagnostic framework (floating button + test panel)
probe = {import:terg-perchance-probe}                 // 🛰 API probe battery (real generateText/generateImage/kv calls)

// --- Page metadata (search engines, social cards, browser tab) ---
$meta
  title = 🍕Beta Berg Story Chat - Long-Form AI RPG
  description = A Open Beta, for testing new features with the comunity

pageTitle = Beta Berg Story Chat


// ============================================================================
// BERG STORY CHAT v1.12.0 — PROJECT HISTORY, DESIGN NOTES & CONTRIBUTOR GUIDE
// ============================================================================
//  P.Berg + gpt + gemini + meta
//
// Berg Story Chat is a community-driven fork built on top of BlizzardUI.
//
// First and foremost, full credit and appreciation go to the original
// BlizzardUI project and everyone who contributed to it.
//
// This project exists because BlizzardUI already contained something rare:
// a surprisingly capable narrative engine hidden beneath a relatively simple
// interface. Many of the foundations required for long-form storytelling
// were already present:
//
// - Streaming text generation
// - Structured state tracking
// - Character and world persistence
// - Image generation support
// - Local-first storage
// - Modular panel architecture
// - Tracker-driven narrative maintenance
//
// The goal of Berg Story Chat was never to replace those foundations.
//
// The goal was to better expose their potential.
//
// Throughout development we repeatedly discovered that many systems already
// existed inside the engine, but were either underutilized, disconnected from
// the user experience, or hidden behind layers of implementation that made
// their value difficult to perceive.
//
// Instead of rebuilding everything, we chose a different approach:
//
// Understand what already exists.
// Preserve what works.
// Simplify what creates friction.
// Expose what creates value.
//
// This philosophy guided nearly every decision made during the evolution of
// this fork.
//
// ============================================================================
// WHAT THIS PROJECT IS NOT
// ============================================================================
//
// Berg Story Chat is not an attempt to turn a narrative experience into a
// dashboard.
//
// It is not a telemetry system.
//
// It is not a roleplaying spreadsheet.
//
// It is not a developer console disguised as a chat application.
//
// One of the most important discoveries during development was that different
// areas of the interface serve fundamentally different psychological purposes.
//
// Mixing those purposes degrades the experience.
//
// Because of that, the project gradually evolved toward a strict separation
// between immersion spaces and information spaces.
//
// ============================================================================
// THE THREE EXPERIENCE ZONES
// ============================================================================
//
// LEFT PANEL
//
// The left panel exists primarily as a comfort zone.
//
// It contains configuration, navigation, saves, imports, exports, shortcuts,
// and utility functions.
//
// Its purpose is not to tell the story.
//
// Its purpose is to help the user manage the environment around the story.
//
// Most users can spend their entire session interacting only with the left
// panel and the chat itself.
//
// The panel should remain approachable, predictable, and low-noise.
//
// Complexity should not accumulate here.
//
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// CENTER PANEL
//
// The center panel is the most protected area of the entire application.
//
// During development we often referred to it as:
//
// "The Sacred Space."
//
// This is where the actual story happens.
//
// It is intentionally linear.
//
// It is intentionally simple.
//
// It is intentionally focused.
//
// The center panel should contain only finalized narrative content:
//
// - Player actions
// - AI responses
// - Generated prose
// - Narrative progression
//
// The center panel should not become a status monitor.
//
// It should not contain:
//
// - Debug information
// - Runtime diagnostics
// - Tracker telemetry
// - State inspection tools
// - Experimental widgets
// - Streaming internals
//
// Every attempt to inject auxiliary information into the central narrative
// flow reduced immersion.
//
// The result was always worse than the clean version.
//
// The story itself is the interface.
//
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// RIGHT PANEL
//
// The right panel serves a completely different purpose.
//
// If the center panel is the story itself,
// the right panel is the world's memory.
//
// This is where the underlying simulation becomes visible.
//
// Unlike the center panel, information density is desirable here.
//
// Advanced users often want visibility into:
//
// - Characters
// - Relationships
// - World state
// - Lore
// - Rules
// - Inventory
// - Narrative history
//
// The right panel exists specifically for that audience.
//
// It is intentionally more information-rich.
//
// The guiding principle became:
//
// "Protect the center. Enrich the right."
//
// ============================================================================
// A MAJOR DISCOVERY: THE TRACKER WAS ALREADY THE ANSWER
// ============================================================================
//
// Several times during development we considered creating entirely new
// systems:
//
// - Story Lens engines
// - Narrative telemetry layers
// - Event buses
// - Context analyzers
// - Regex-based extraction systems
//
// After investigating the architecture more deeply, we realized most of
// those ideas would only duplicate functionality that already existed.
//
// The tracker pipeline was already doing the difficult work.
//
// Background AI processes were already extracting:
//
// - Character information
// - World information
// - Lore
// - Relationships
// - Narrative summaries
//
// The problem was not missing intelligence.
//
// The problem was missing projection.
//
// Valuable information was being generated but not being shown.
//
// Once this became clear, the design direction changed completely.
//
// Instead of building new systems,
// we focused on exposing the outputs of existing systems.
//
// This reduced complexity,
// reduced maintenance cost,
// reduced failure points,
// and improved transparency simultaneously.
//
// ============================================================================
// THE CHRONICLE DISCOVERY
// ============================================================================
//
// One particularly important example involved the story seed.
//
// The initial story premise ("overview") is one of the most important pieces
// of information in the entire simulation.
//
// It acts as the narrative DNA of the world.
//
// However, once generation began, that information effectively disappeared
// from the interface.
//
// The engine still used it.
//
// The user simply could not see it anymore.
//
// Similar situations existed with:
//
// - Story summaries
// - Narrative progression
// - Historical context
//
// The Chronicle section emerged from a simple question:
//
// If the engine already knows these things,
// why hide them?
//
// The Chronicle therefore became a projection layer for information that was
// already stable, already maintained, and already useful.
//
// No new intelligence was added.
//
// Visibility was added.
//
// ============================================================================
// STATE DESIGN LESSONS
// ============================================================================
//
// One of the most important architectural lessons learned during development
// involved state visibility.
//
// Not all state should be visible.
//
// The engine naturally produces two categories of information:
//
// 1. TRANSIENT STATE
// 2. COMMITTED STATE
//
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// TRANSIENT STATE
//
// Examples:
//
// - Streaming chunks
// - Token buffers
// - Intermediate parser outputs
// - In-progress generations
// - Temporary prompts
// - Active requests
//
// These values are volatile.
//
// They exist only while work is being performed.
//
// Rendering them directly creates:
//
// - Flickering
// - Race conditions
// - Visual instability
// - Inconsistent information
//
// The UI should avoid consuming these values whenever possible.
//
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// COMMITTED STATE
//
// Examples:
//
// - state.overview
// - state.summary
// - state.turns
// - state.tracker
//
// These values represent information that has already survived the
// generation pipeline and become part of the world's stable reality.
//
// These values are safe.
//
// These values are useful.
//
// These values should drive interface projections.
//
// Over time this became one of the core design principles of the project.
//
// Stable state informs UI.
// Volatile state remains internal.
//
// ============================================================================
// CONCURRENCY, STREAMING & HARD-LEARNED LESSONS
// ============================================================================
//
// During development we encountered multiple edge cases involving streaming,
// asynchronous generation, iframe communication, and Perchance's internal
// AI transport layer.
//
// Several early solutions relied on aggressive browser-level workarounds.
//
// Those solutions appeared effective initially,
// but introduced hidden risks.
//
// Over time we moved away from:
//
// - Global DOM monkeypatches
// - Prototype overrides
// - Browser API substitutions
//
// and toward:
//
// - Scheduling
// - Serialization
// - Grace periods
// - Queue management
// - Non-invasive diagnostics
//
// The resulting system became both simpler and more reliable.
//
// The lesson was straightforward:
//
// If a browser API must be rewritten globally to solve a problem,
// the problem is probably being solved at the wrong layer.
//
// ============================================================================
// UI EVOLUTION
// ============================================================================
//
// Another recurring theme throughout development was the removal of
// redundancy.
//
// Multiple interface elements performed identical functions.
//
// Some occupied permanent screen space while providing little value.
//
// Rather than adding more interface,
// we repeatedly found value by removing interface.
//
// The top bar is an example of this process.
//
// Over time its responsibilities migrated naturally into the side panels,
// allowing more vertical space for the actual story.
//
// Similar decisions shaped much of the current layout.
//
// The objective was never minimalism for its own sake.
//
// The objective was focus.
//
// Every persistent UI element should justify the space it occupies.
//
// ============================================================================
// FOR FUTURE CONTRIBUTORS
// ============================================================================
//
// If you are reading this because you plan to modify, extend, fork,
// or experiment with Berg Story Chat:
//
// Start by understanding what already exists.
//
// Before adding:
//
// - A new state system
// - A new tracker
// - A new event layer
// - A new memory structure
// - A new synchronization pipeline
//
// first verify whether the engine already contains that capability.
//
// More often than expected,
// the answer is yes.
//
// The project improved most when existing systems were clarified,
// exposed,
// refined,
// and connected together.
//
// Not when they were replaced.
//
// If you decide to build on top of this work,
// we hope these notes help you understand not only what the system does,
// but why it evolved the way it did.
//
// And if you discover a cleaner, simpler, or more elegant solution,
// please improve it.
//
// That possibility is the entire reason this document exists.
//
// Thank you for taking the time to explore the project.
//
// And thank you again to the original BlizzardUI creators for building the
// foundation that made all of this possible.
// ============================================================================// ==========================================================================
//   📜 BERGSTORYCHAT — CORE ARCHITECTURAL ENGINE & UI LAYER SPECIFICATION
//   ==========================================================================
//   
//   This specification defines the separation of cognitive loads, state truth,
//   and rendering pipelines within BergStoryChat. It serves as the living
//   blueprint for future iterations, preventing state drift and regression.
//
//   1. THREE-TIERED COGNITIVE EXPERIENCE ZONES
//   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   ( LEFT PANEL: COMFORT & WORKSPACE )
//   - Objective: Casual player sanctuary.
//   - Responsibilities: Low-noise navigation, theme controls, local saves, 
//     import/export tools, and configuration settings.
//   - Psychological Goal: Establishes baseline stability, trust, and light reading.
//
//   ( CENTER PANEL: THE SACRED LINEAR FLOW )
//   - Objective: Clean, immersive, distraction-free prose.
//   - Rules: Strictly contains finalized AI and Player conversational turns.
//     Absolutely no floating HUD, no telemetry logs, no mid-generation drafts,
//     and no real-time stream status indicators.
//   - Psychological Goal: Maintains maximum focus on pure narrative immersion.
//
//   ( RIGHT PANEL: THE COGNITIVE WORLD BRAIN )
//   - Objective: Structured insight into the simulation's underlying state.
//   - Responsibilities: Renders stable, reconciled state projections.
//   - Key Modules:
//     * CHRONICLE: Displays raw seed (Overview), narrative chronology (Beats),
//       and consolidated memory (Core Summary).
//       We map overview through data-edit="overview" to openWelcome() directly,
//       and bind narrative age to state.turns.length and state.summary.
//     * CAST: Identifies active NPCs and their core backgrounds.
//     * LORE: Lists canonized world rules, facts, relationships, and active threads.
//   - Psychological Goal: Exposes the cognitive "neural clockwork" of the world,
//     allowing the player to see that the universe actively breathes and adapts.
//
//   2. DUAL-LAYER STATE ARCHITECTURE (UNIDIRECTIONAL PROPAGATION)
//   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The system maintains a strict separation of timelines and state integrity:
//
//   ( PHASE 1: TRANSIENT COGNITIVE FLOW (VOLATILE/UNCOMMITTED) )
//   - Elements: Streaming chunks, raw token buffers, active promise handlers,
//     intermediary memory extraction parses, and temporary generation parameters.
//   - Access Rule: UI IS STRICTLY FORBIDDEN FROM READING OR RENDER-POLLING THIS LAYER.
//     This prevents rendering flickers, racing conditions, and premature evaluations.
//
//   ( PHASE 2: COMMITTED STATE (STABLE/UI-SAFE SNAPSHOT) )
//   - Elements: `state.overview` (identity), `state.turns` (time), `state.summary`
//     (memory), and `state.tracker` (structured truth).
//   - Access Rule: This is the singular source of truth for all UI render cycles.
//     Projections are triggered exclusively via state callbacks (e.g., renderState)
//     at established transaction boundaries.
//
//   3. SYSTEM FLOW & STATE UPDATES
//   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   A single game turn (Beat) executes through a rigid transactional pipeline:
//   
//   ( INPUT ) ---> ( CONTEXT BUILD ) ---> ( AI GENERATION ) ---> ( STREAM TO CHAT (PROSE ONLY) )
//                                                                           |
//   ( UI RENDER ) <--- ( STATE COMMIT ) <--- ( TRACKER MERGE ) <--- ( NARRATIVE FRAME CLOSE )
//          |
//          +--> ( BACKGROUND COMPRESSION / maybeSummarize() ) ---> ( LAZY MEMORY EVENT )
//
//   - Real-Time stream rendering is isolated entirely to the chat view container.
//   - The Right Panel and Tracker are re-evaluated and re-rendered only when
//     reconciled, finalized transaction frames are committed.
//
//   4. WELCOME GUIDE & NEW SYSTEMS INTEGRATION (DEVELOPER MINDSET)
//   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Welcome to the BergStoryChat source code! This project is maintained by the
//   community as a living hybrid. Our goal when expanding this ecosystem was to
//   close the data loop without creating "ghost systems" (decorative information 
//   that doesn't actually influence the game simulation) and to preserve the
//   Perchance DSL parser and intrinsic performance as much as possible.
//
//   We dedicate this guide to all new programmers and creators who wish to understand,
//   maintain, or modularize the new integrated mechanisms of atmosphere, image generation,
//   on-demand inspection, and isolated telemetry.
//
//   ==========================================================================
//   (A) CLIMATOLOGY ENGINE & ENVIRONMENTAL METABOLISM
//   ==========================================================================
//   - CONCEPT:
//     The emotional and physical climate of the world must not be a dead statistic on a panel.
//     It acts as an active narrative barometer, working as a "background pressure"
//     that influences both the tone of subsequent language model responses and the
//     lighting and optical direction generated by the visual compiler lenses.
//
//   - HOW IT WORKS (TECHNICALLY):
//     The system operates autonomously at the end of each turn ("Beat") via:
//     * updateWorldMetrics(tracker, recentText): Analyzes recent prose using Sensory
//       Pattern Heuristics. It scans the text for semantic markers of conflict/tension
//       (e.g., weapons, wounds, danger), calm (e.g., taverns, peace, rest), mystical
//       elements (e.g., temples, mysticism, ritual, god, faith, prayer, vision, relic),
//       or economic elements (e.g., coins, gold, buying, selling, market, merchant) and
//       translates these readings into instant pressures on world metrics.
//     * Natural Metabolism (Amortization): To avoid permanent hysterical extremes,
//       the engine decays and silently regenerates each metric toward healthy baseline
//       equilibrium values with each world-tick, allowing the "dust to settle" naturally.
//     * validateWorldIntegrity(tracker): Acts as the "structural immune system" of the save.
//       It ensures all gauges respect the (0.0 to 1.0) scale, sanitizes old or legacy
//       states, and prevents type mismatches (such as NaN or nulls) during import.
//
//   ==========================================================================
//   (B) SEMANTIC VISUAL STATE ENGINE (VSE V3) / VISUAL COMPILER
//   ==========================================================================
//   - CONCEPT:
//     Traditionally, image generators in RPGs draw simplistic conclusions from the last sentence 
//     of the story, losing the sense of continuity of who is on scene and the physical environment. 
//     Visual Compiler V3 solves this by structuring realities in a unified way for the image plugin,
//     ensuring the prompt sent to the engine is a cinematic piece.
//
//   - HOW IT WORKS (TECHNICALLY):
//     When triggered, VisualCompilerV3 scans data along 3 axes:
//     * Active Subject Resolution: The compiler detects who is present in the turn's scene
//       and queries the NPCs' Visual Genome (resolveNpcAppearance / visualGenomeSystem).
//       It extracts real physical features from their JSON (their morphology, active clothing
//       pieces, documented scars), ignoring behavioral fields that would pollute the prompt
//       and applying weight-based salience rules.
//     * Atmosphere Optics (Atmospheric lighting derived from weather): The physical atmosphere
//       generated in the image prompt is directly linked to the Climatology Engine. If world
//       Tension is high or Fear rises (tension > 0.65 or fear > 0.60), the compiler dictates
//       aggressive lighting directions (heavy chiaroscuro shadows, high dramatic contrast, somber tones).
//       If the world is stable (stability > 0.75), it dictates soft natural light and "golden hour"
//       illumination.
//     * Attention Bridge & Memory Echoes: The visual compiler establishes a context bridge with
//       the Memory Palace. It scans the scene's text for keywords and looks for recently activated
//       related memories. If they exist, it subtly injects "visual whispers and echoes" of those
//       background memories into the structured scene description, guaranteeing subjective continuity
//       in images across turns (Attention Bridge).
//
//   ==========================================================================
//   (C) SPLIT-BUTTON IMAGING UI & PROMPT INSPECTOR
//   ==========================================================================
//   - CONCEPT:
//     The player's immersion is "The Sacred Space". No player should be forced to deal with
//     technical sheets, complex dialogue boxes, or raw prompts in the middle of the screen
//     while just playing the game. However, experienced creators and writers need on-demand
//     oversight tools.
//
//   - HOW IT WORKS (TECHNICALLY):
//     We redesigned the generation interface in the footer (#Ebb) using a smart split button
//     (.qbtn-group): Main Button (Qimage) | Side Arrow (Qimage-arrow).
//     * Direct Generation (Quick Click): Runs the compiler in an invisible, low-friction
//       automatic mode. The player clicks, the prompt compiles silently in the background,
//       and the image begins loading in the main feed without opening modals or pausing the text RPG flow.
//     * On-Demand Inspector (Synchronous Arrow): Provides access to the Prompt Inspector. It
//       is loaded in "lazy" mode (preventing render locks during initialization) and cleanly
//       exposes the visual compiler layers without heavy background blur filters (friendly blur),
//       allowing the writer to audit, edit, and change the bias focus before sending.
//
//   ==========================================================================
//   (D) DECOUPLED LOG VIEWER & ZERO MEMORY OVERHEAD
//   ==========================================================================
//   - CONCEPT:
//     Trying to debug complex pipelines in production usually leads to two disasters: either we are
//     completely blind to what the system is sending, or we pollute the DOM and browser memory
//     monitoring heavy string data in real-time, causing thrashing.
//
//   - HOW IT WORKS (TECHNICALLY):
//     We implemented a Single-Entry Router Logger (logDebug or structured console log) of 3 points:
//     1. LOG 1 - Real Cognitive State: The true state of the world tracker and active incoming heroes.
//     2. LOG 2 - Compiled Output Resolution: The processed result of subjects, atmosphere, and memory echoes.
//     3. LOG 3 - Final Image Plugin Prompt: The final textual payload sent directly to Perchance tools.
//     This direct mapping allows any developer to audit the flow without degrading game runtime
//     performance in any way, keeping files lightly organized.
//
//   ==========================================================================
//   (E) METABOLIC PHYSIOLOGY & METABOLIC SCHEDULER (V4)
//   ==========================================================================
//   - CONCEPT:
//     The system has migrated from an actions flow based purely on imperative and statistical
//     code ("X turns passed, so run Y") to a METABOLIC PHYSIOLOGY DRIVEN BY THERMODYNAMIC PULSES.
//     Information now possesses a real life-cycle: it is born, matures, circulates, ages, dies,
//     or fossilizes.
//
//   - THE COMMON COGNITIVE DNA (Constitution vs. Role):
//     To eliminate prompt redundancy and inconsistencies after hundreds of turns, LLMs are
//     no longer controlled by force with long lists of redundant prohibitions in each call.
//     Instead, we teach a SINGLE COMMON COGNITIVE DNA (the "Constitution"):
//       * GROUND TRUTH: Base actions only on observable, canonical facts of the story.
//       * NO SPECULATION: Never invent events or states that did not occur in the prose.
//       * NO DUPLICATES: Do not repeat or inflate already consolidated facts.
//       * ALLOW DECAY / ALLOW REPLACEMENT (SUPERSEDE): Allow data to age and be superseded.
//       * RETURN STRUCTURED DATA: Provide clean, parseable outputs.
//     After injecting this central DNA, each agent assumes its specific "Role" of responsibility:
//       * Active Memory (The Tracker): The "Skin of the Present" (Immediate states and resources).
//       * Episodic Memory (The Summary): The "Historical Causality / Hippocampus" (Chains and connections of cause and effect).
//       * Long-Term Memory (The Memory Palace): The "Fossils of the Past / Palace" (Crystallization of high-gravity facts).
//
//   - O METABOLIC SCHEDULER & NARRATIVE GRAVITY:
//     The Scheduler does not execute cognitive calls based on raw time or token counts. It
//     computes the GRAVITY of each action, converting narrative events into a continuous
//     thermodynamic flow:
//       * Semantic Mass Heuristic (computeSemanticMass): Analyzes recent text for keywords.
//         Small events (NPC smiled) accumulate near-zero mass (~0.5). High-tension events,
//         combat, deaths, discoveries, or explicit oaths explode mass instantly (from +3.0 to +6.0).
//       * Metabolic Energy Accumulation (state.metabolicEnergy): Each turn adds this mass into dynamic accumulators.
//       * Thermodynamic Thresholds: When pressure reaches the critical point in each channel, a metabolic pulse
//         is fired, triggering the corresponding organ to metabolize and compress the information:
//           - Tracker (Skin of the Present): Fires when pressure reaches `trackerInterval * 2.0` (by default, ~10.0 mass).
//           - Memory Palace (The Fossils): Requires high-gravity events, firing when reaching `T_palace = 8.0` energy.
//
//   ==========================================================================
//   (F) THE PRISANTENMOTRIA OBSERVABILITY SYSTEM (SINGLE STREAM)
//   ==========================================================================
//   - CONCEPT:
//     To allow deep runtime tracking of the narrative brain without introducing 
//     memory leakage or polluting the Sacred Immersion Space, the system introduces 
//     the "Prisantenmotria" unified observability standard.
//
//   - HOW IT WORKS (TECHNICALLY):
//     * Single Observability Stream (window.obsStream): A high-precision, low-overhead
//       in-memory ring buffer (capped at 200 items to avoid memory thrashing) that captures 
//       every vital state shift, cognitive interpretation, and text/visual generation event.
//     * Unified Emitter (window.emitObs(origin, category, payload)): A standardized, 
//       safe emitter that checks `window.observabilityEnabled` and filters events along three
//       distinct channels:
//         - 'state': Track transitions, state serialization, manual and quick saves, 
//           tracker sanitation passes, and life-cycle events (e.g. `parasitic_boot_hydrated`).
//         - 'interpretation': Audits semantic extractions, Memory Palace lookups, 
//           weathering computations, and context-assembly logic.
//         - 'generation': Captures the active prompt-building schemas, LLM parameter injections, 
//           and generated visual specs.
//     * UI Observability Panel: An optional, developer-oriented live stream visualizer 
//       integrated cleanly into the left navigation panel (`renderLogsTab()`), allowing 
//       real-time debugging without console or DOM clutter.
//
//   ==========================================================================
//   (G) DETERMINISTIC INVENTORY & ITEM PHYSIOLOGY
//   ==========================================================================
//   - CONCEPT:
//     In long-form RPG simulations, items and resources are not merely passive labels—they
//     constrain and shape what actions the character can realistically take. Therefore, the 
//     inventory parser must operate with deterministic mathematical rules, eliminating 
//     "immortal consumables" and providing structured charge mechanics.
//
//   - HOW IT WORKS (TECHNICALLY):
//     * The Zero-Quantity Purge: A critical bug resolved in the item normalizer prevents
//       items from persisting forever at quantity 0 (where older logic like `parseInt(item.qty) || 1`
//       would incorrectly raise 0 to 1). The system now strictly guarantees that if a 
//       consumable is depleted to `qty: 0`, it is fully purged from the active tracker state, 
//       preventing immortal resources.
//     * Parenthetical Uses Parser: The item normalizer scans item names for charge sub-patterns, 
//       such as `Poção (2/3)`. It maps these patterns into explicit, structured object properties:
//         - `uses`: 2 (active remaining charges)
//         - `maxUses`: 3 (maximum physical capacity)
//       This structured representation allows the simulation to deplete individual item charges 
//       smoothly across turns without breaking state consistency or losing the base item name.
//
//   ==========================================================================
//   (H) HYBRID PERSISTENCE ENGINE & COMPATIBILITY MODE
//   ==========================================================================
//   - CONCEPT:
//     In certain environments (particularly iOS Safari with ITP blocking in iframes, 
//     Firefox RFP, or private windows), IndexedDB fails catastrophically or is completely blocked. 
//     This previously prevented users from saving their journeys or caused total data loss upon refreshing (F5).
//     The Hybrid Persistence Engine resolves this by verifying database availability 
//     (via Dexie) and, if it detects failures or a hostile environment (such as iOS inside an iframe), 
//     establishes a safe and resilient escape route to `localStorage`.
//
//   - HOW IT WORKS (TECHNICALLY):
//     * Proactive Environment Checking: The system detects if the client is an iOS device 
//       running inside an iframe (`forceLocal = isIOS && isInIframe`). If true, or if 
//       Dexie fails to open the database instance ("BergStoryChat"), the engine sets 
//       `useLocalFallback = true`.
//     * Robust Storage Routing (getLocalSaves / saveStoryDataToUsersDevice): All read 
//       and write operations asynchronously query the primary database. If unavailable, 
//       the flow is transparently redirected to the serialized `localStorage` key.
//     * Smart Quota Mitigation (purgeHeavyData): To bypass the strict 5MB limit of 
//       `localStorage`, the engine intercepts the record before saving in compatibility mode 
//       and removes redundant and heavy binary or base64 data (clearing the visualizer image gallery), 
//       ensuring that long-form narratives of hundreds of turns remain lightweight (~1.8MB) and fit comfortably in the quota.
//     * Visual Warnings & Export Call-to-Action: Upon the first failure or fallback activation, the user 
//       is alerted once via a persistent warning toast ("Modo Compatibilidade: Exporte seus saves!") 
//       while a pulsing animation (`.flash-export`) is applied to the export button, encouraging them to 
//       maintain local, portable backup copies in .json files.
//
//   ==========================================================================
//   (I) COERCIVE DATA PARSING & AGGRESSIVE IMPORT DECODER
//   ==========================================================================
//   - CONCEPT:
//     Users frequently move between different AI roleplaying frontends (such as SillyTavern,
//     Agnai, character.ai, or custom setups) and expect to import their existing chat histories
//     smoothly. A rigid importer causes massive user frustration. The Coercive Data Parsing engine
//     resolves this by automatically detecting, parsing, and converting foreign structural schemas
//     into BergStoryChat's native state structure.
//
//   - HOW IT WORKS (TECHNICALLY):
//     * Broad Container Recognition: The improved `coerceImportedData()` detects structured turns 
//       from standard imports or raw chat log arrays, checking common properties like `.history`, 
//       `.chatHistory`, `.chat_history`, `.logs`, `.scenes`, `.conversations`, or `.chats`.
//     * Recursive Deep-Search Decoder (`deepFindMessageArray`): If standard containers are missing, 
//       a recursive scan traverses nested JSON keys up to 6 levels deep. It looks for arrays 
//       containing message-like objects (possessing keys like `mes`, `message`, `content`, `text_content`, 
//       `text`, or `body`) or raw string lists.
//     * Coercive Conversational Converter: If an array of raw strings is found via deep search, the 
//       system coerces them into structured turns, alternating the sender's role sequentially between
//       user and ai (e.g. `is_user` toggling) to construct a clean chronological narrative feed.
//     * Interactive Telemetry Debugger (`BERG_DEBUG`): Users and developers can activate diagnostic 
//       logging by executing `localStorage.setItem('BERG_DEBUG', 'true')` in their browser console. 
//       This prompts step-by-step console messages of the coercion pipeline, simplifying the audit of custom structures.
//
//   ==========================================================================
//   (J) LEGACY SAVE MIGRATION ENGINE (INDEXEDDB -> LOCALSTORAGE)
//   ==========================================================================
//   - CONCEPT:
//     Upgrades can sometimes create silent storage discrepancies. When the hybrid compatibility layer 
//     was introduced to force localStorage on sandboxed iOS environments, users with older saves 
//     stored in IndexedDB suddenly faced an empty dashboard. To prevent save loss and community 
//     frustration, a fail-safe background migration bridge is required to transparently recover 
//     legacy data.
//
//   - HOW IT WORKS (TECHNICALLY):
//     * One-Time Execution Sentinel: During boot (inside `onUIReady()`), the migration routine 
//       checks for the `BERG_MIGRATED_V1` local storage key. If set to `'true'`, it skips execution,
//       ensuring zero performance or database overhead for subsequent sessions.
//       This prevents double-execution or unwanted merges.
//     * Silent IndexedDB Recovery (migrateLegacyIndexedDB): The system initiates a non-blocking 
//       open promise to read the old "BergStoryChat" Dexie database. If present, it loads all legacy 
//       saves.
//     * Non-Destructive Merging: It parses existing localStorage saves under `SAVE_DB_KEY` and merges
//       only those legacy saves whose unique IDs do not already exist in localStorage. This preserves 
//       current progress while pulling missing historical campaigns.
//     * Double-Buffered Safety: It never deletes old IndexedDB records, maintaining them as 
//       untouched copies for user security. Once merged, it compresses the updated database using `LZSimple`,
//       marks `BERG_MIGRATED_V1 = true`, and issues a beautiful success toast alerting the user to the 
//       number of stories safely recovered.
//
//   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Be humble in your creations, protect the Sacred Central Immersion Space, and use Telemetry
//   to keep the gears of this wonderful organism clean, organized, and glowing! Thank you
//   for continuing to build on top of this fortress.
//   ==========================================================================
//
//
//   ==========================================================================
//   (K) SPECIAL THANKS
//   ==========================================================================
//    - Teophrast
//    - TinyTerribleDragon
//    - DigitalDisgust
//    - FlatulentGlitterBees
//    - Glittering-Move-220
