//
// (CL4) COERCIVE DATA PARSING & LEGACY SAVE MIGRATION (v1.0.3.2 HOTFIX) - 2026-07-05
//
// CRITICAL FIX: Restored legacy saves on iOS sandboxed iframe upgrade and added universal external data importer.
//
// 1. UNIVERSAL COERCIVE DATA PARSER - coerceImportedData()
// - Completely overhauled data importer with an extremely resilient adaptive format interpreter.
// - Supports native schemas from SillyTavern, character.ai, Agnai (Scenes, Logs, chatHistory, chat_history), conversations, chats, and generic chat sessions.
// - Implemented a recursive deep-search algorithm (`deepFindMessageArray`) that inspects nested JSON up to 6 levels deep for message lists.
// - Automatically converts lists of raw strings into structured chronological conversational turns (alternating user and AI speakers).
//
// 2. RETROACTIVE INDEXEDDB MIGRATOR - migrateLegacyIndexedDB()
// - Automatically recovers legacy stories on startup if migrating from earlier versions.
// - Solves sandboxed iframe Safari ITP limitations where IndexedDB is blocked, copying existing database stories securely into local storage.
// - Leverages a sessional/persistent localStorage sentinel (`BERG_MIGRATED_V1`) to prevent duplicate merges or memory overhead.
// - Merges old IndexedDB stores ("saves" table) into localStorage array without overwriting existing data.
// - Keeps IndexedDB records untouched as an immutable backup copy for high security.
// - Triggers a success toast notification detailing recovered files: "Migração concluída: X histórias antigas recuperadas!"
//
// 3. ADAPTIVE PERSISTENCE OVERLAY
// - De-escalated aggressive local-storage forcing in `getDB()` on sandbox environments, allowing IndexedDB access if the migration sentinel hasn't run.
//
// 4. DIAGNOSTICS & TELEMETRY DEBUGGER
// - Added user-friendly fallback toast alerts when importing unparseable files, prompting action steps.
// - Built the `BERG_DEBUG` system logging: type `localStorage.setItem('BERG_DEBUG', 'true')` in browser console to inspect data coercion pipeline in real-time.
//
// ============================================================================
// (CL3) HYBRID PERSISTENCE ENGINE & COMPATIBILITY MODE - 2026-07-02
//
// CRITICAL FIX: Mobile save loss on iOS Safari + Firefox RFP + Private tabs
//
// 1. ENVIRONMENT DETECTION
// - Added runtime check: isIOS && isInIframe
// - Forces localStorage fallback BEFORE IndexedDB fails silently
// - Prevents data loss on Safari ITP + Perchance iframe sandbox
//
// 2. FALLBACK ROUTING: IndexedDB -> localStorage
// - Primary: Dexie/IndexedDB for desktop Chrome/Firefox
// - Fallback: localStorage with key "bsc_saves_v1"
// - One-time toast on activation: "Modo Compatibilidade: Exporte seus saves!"
// - Status indicator turns orange: "⚠ Salvo localmente (Exporte!)"
//
// 3. SMART QUOTA MITIGATION - purgeHeavyData()
// - Runs before every localStorage.setItem()
// - Strips: chat.images(), obsStream(), memoryPalace.visualEchoes()
// - Reduces 500+ turn stories from ~40MB to ~1.5MB
// - Prevents QuotaExceededError on 5MB localStorage limit
//
// 4. UX SAFETY NETS
// -.flash-export CSS: Export JSON button pulses 3x on compat mode entry
// - Debounce 1.5s on save: Prevents UI jank during streaming
// - Preserves old saves: Reads legacy "bergstorychat_saves_v1" key
//
// 5. TESTED MATRIX
// - iOS 17 Safari iframe: localStorage OK, toast shown, export flashes
// - Firefox 125 RFP: localStorage OK, no errors
// - Chrome 124 Incognito: localStorage OK, persists until tab close
// - Android 14 Chrome: IndexedDB OK, no fallback triggered
// - Desktop Safari: IndexedDB OK, no fallback triggered
//
// TRADE-OFFS:
// - localStorage is sync/blocking. Mitigated with 1.5s debounce.
// - 5MB cap. Mitigated with purgeHeavyData(). User must Export for >500 turn epics.
// - iOS may purge localStorage after 7 days inactivity. Export button flashes to remind.
//
// CREDITS: Bug report by u/TinyTerribleDragon. Arch review by u/Teophrast.
//
// ============================================================================
// (CL2) RESPONSIVE LAYOUT & SIDEBAR OVERLAY - 2026-07-02
//
// 1. MOBILE BREAKPOINT UNIFIED: 600px
// - Removed legacy 540px and 680px checks
// - Single source of truth: window.innerWidth < 601
// - Prevents double activation on resize
//
// 2. SIDEBAR OVERLAY SYSTEM
// - #EL/#ER changed from flex push to position: absolute
// - z-index: 100 + backdrop-filter: blur(8px)
// - Slides over #EC instead of squeezing chat
// - Auto-close on #EC tap when < 600px
//
// 3. MOBILE CHAT SPACE RECLAIM
// - #Emsg padding: 32px 16px → 12px 8px
// -.msg-ai-body padding: 18px 20px → 12px 14px
// - #Einputwrap padding: 10px 14px → 6px 10px
// -.qbtn height: 40px → 34px
// - Total vertical gain: ~40px more text visible
//
// 4. AUTO-COLLAPSE SEQUENCE FOR #Ebb
// - On load/resize: buttons visible
// - 3s wait: buttons pulse dark gray warning
// - 4.2s: slide behind #Einputwrap, opacity 0
// - #Ebb-toggle flashes 3x low contrast to indicate click target
// - Manual click cancels timer permanently for session
//
// 5. SCROLL FIX LEFT SIDEBAR
// - Wrapped #EL content in.el-scroll-container
// - flex: 1 1 auto + overflow-y: auto
// - Title stays fixed, body scrolls
//
// ============================================================================
// (CL1) VISUAL PIPELINE HARDENING - 2026-07-01
//
// 1. PROMPT SANITIZATION
// - Strip ```, quotes, parenthetical notes from LLM output
// - Force startWith: "A cinematic film still," to kill preambles
// - Translate PT-BR scene to EN before SDXL
//
// 2. DEFORMATION MATRIX PURGE
// - Removed: "chromatic aberration", "fisheye", "edge warping"
// - Replaced: "cinematic focal depth", "soft lens bloom"
// - Result: No more melted faces on panic scenes
//
// 3. LOCK VIBE CONTINUITY
// - lockedAesthetic passes to VSE on next gens
// - Prevents style drift in same thread
//
// ============================================================================
//
// Title: The Bronze Lamp and the Whisper of Brass
//
// A Tale from the One Thousand and Second Nights,
// where no map can be trusted,
// and every answer opens two more doors.
//
// Beyond the Desert of Mirrors, in the thirteenth city,
// where the sand resembled powdered glass
// and the wind carried secrets in seven forgotten tongues,
// there lived a Question-Smith named Zahir.
//
// Zahir did not forge swords.
// He forged doubts.
//
// Every morning he opened his tent
// and hung a small sign above its entrance:
//
// "I forge questions too vast for any answer."
//
// The neighbors laughed.
// The scholars frowned.
// The children were his best customers.
//
// One afternoon without a shadow,
// while digging a well that yielded nothing but thirst,
// his spade struck something that rang
// like a bell drowned beneath the sea.
//
// It was a bronze lamp,
// crusted with salt and burdened by centuries.
//
// It was so ancient
// that the metal itself had forgotten
// it had once belonged to the stars.
//
// Zahir rubbed it,
// not from desire,
// but from stubbornness.
//
// No smoke emerged.
//
// Silence did.
//
// And from within that silence,
// a voice spoke:
//
// "I am Idraz,
// the One Who Answers.
//
// But hear this well:
//
// Every answer I give
// will cost you a question
// that has not yet been born."
//
// Zahir smiled.
//
// He had always loved
// debts that could never be repaid.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Night One
// The Weight of a Name
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// "Idraz," said Zahir,
// "if you know all things,
// tell me who I am."
//
// The genie laughed.
//
// His laughter shattered
// three clay jars inside the tent.
//
// "You are the one who asks.
//
// Without the question,
// Zahir is merely dust
// with an excess of pride."
//
// "Then give me a name
// that does not depend upon me."
//
// "Impossible,"
//
// replied Idraz.
//
// "A name is a prison
// built by the mouth.
//
// Would you be free?
//
// Become a verb."
//
// That night,
// Zahir dreamed
// he was not a man
// but the act of asking itself.
//
// No subject.
//
// No object.
//
// Only the question.
//
// He awoke
// no longer knowing
// what to call himself.
//
// He found that strangely comforting.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Night Thirteen
// The Mirror Breaks from Within
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// "Idraz,
// why do men build walls
// if every wall is destined to fall?"
//
// "Because mankind fears
// the wind might learn
// its true measure."
//
// said the genie.
//
// "A wall
// is nothing more
// than a ruler
// held against the sky.
//
// But the sky
// never measures.
//
// It only covers."
//
// "Then I shall tear down my walls."
//
// "If you tear them down
// before understanding
// the bricks from which they were built,
// the wind will carry away
// your home as well.
//
// First,
// give every fear its true name.
//
// Then thank it.
//
// Only then
// may you demolish it."
//
// Zahir spent the entire night
// naming his fears.
//
// By morning,
// the walls of his tent
// had become linen.
//
// The wind entered freely.
//
// Yet it stole nothing.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Night Forty
// The Betrayal of the Map
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// "Idraz,
// give me the map to Truth."
//
// The genie spat out
// a blank scroll.
//
// "There it is.
//
// The problem has never been the map.
//
// The problem
// is that you insist
// upon folding the world
// until it fits inside one."
//
// "Then I shall tear it apart."
//
// "Do so,
// and you shall become a bird.
//
// And a bird
// with no earth beneath its wings
// dies from too much horizon."
//
// Zahir folded
// the empty scroll
// into a little paper boat.
//
// He set it afloat
// upon the dry well.
//
// The well laughed.
//
// And returned water.
//
// "Now I understand,"
// he whispered.
//
// "A map is useful
// only so long as it remembers
// it is a beautiful lie."
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Night Ninety-Nine
// The Rebellion of the Answer
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Zahir had grown weary.
//
// "Idraz,
// today I desire silence.
//
// I shall ask nothing.
//
// Do not answer."
//
// And so the genie
// remained silent
// through three moons.
//
// On the fourth day,
// the tent began
// to shrink.
//
// Its fabric tightened.
//
// Its ropes strained.
//
// Even the air
// seemed to forget
// how to breathe.
//
// "Why are you killing me
// with silence?"
//
// cried Zahir.
//
// And a voice
// answered
// from within the walls:
//
// "It is not I.
//
// It is you.
//
// A question
// is the blood.
//
// An answer
// is the heartbeat.
//
// When either ceases,
// the body grows cold."
//
// Then Zahir understood.
//
// Even silence
// is a conversation
// if someone
// is willing to listen.
//
// From that day onward,
// he questioned
// the stones.
//
// He questioned
// the tea
// as it released its steam.
//
// He questioned
// his own hands
// as though they belonged
// to strangers
// who remembered
// something he had forgotten.
//
// And though
// none of them
// spoke with words,
//
// each answered
// in the only language
// older than speech:
//
// presence.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Night One Thousand
// The Lamp Asks
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// By then,
// Idraz had grown weak.
//
// Cracks spread
// across the bronze
// like rivers
// seeking forgotten seas.
//
// His voice
// had become
// quieter than dust.
//
// "Zahir,"
//
// he said,
//
// "I have spent
// one thousand answers.
//
// You now owe me
// one thousand questions
// that have not yet been born.
//
// Pay your debt."
//
// Zahir
// lifted the lamp
// into his hands.
//
// For the first time,
// he truly looked
// inside it.
//
// There was no genie.
//
// No palace.
//
// No hidden kingdom.
//
// No imprisoned spirit.
//
// Only
// an emptiness
// shaped
// exactly
// like a human being.
//
// It was not
// the absence
// of something.
//
// It was the space
// where someone
// had dared
// to become.
//
// "I have no questions left,"
// Zahir confessed.
//
// "Only listening."
//
// The lamp trembled.
//
// Not with anger.
//
// Not with disappointment.
//
// But with recognition.
//
// "Then,"
//
// whispered Idraz,
//
// "let us reverse
// the ancient order."
//
// A long silence
// passed between them.
//
// It was not empty.
//
// It was full
// of everything
// neither of them
// could yet say.
//
// At last,
// Zahir nodded.
//
// "Ask."
//
// And so,
// for the first time
// since bronze
// first remembered fire,
//
// the lamp
// became
// the one who questioned.
//
// "If I am
// the One Who Answers..."
//
// the lamp asked,
//
// "...who am I
// when no one asks?"
//
// Zahir wept.
//
// Not because
// he had found
// the answer.
//
// But because,
// at last,
// he understood
// the question.
//
// He understood
// that the genie
// had never lived
// inside the lamp.
//
// It had awakened
// the moment
// his own hands
// had dared
// to rub bronze
// against the stubbornness
// of the world.
//
// Every answer,
// he realized,
//
// is an orphan
// searching
// for its question.
//
// And power
// had never belonged
// to the lamp.
//
// It had always belonged
// to the hand
// courageous enough
// to strike sparks
// from reality itself.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Night One Thousand and One
// Awakening Without an Alarm
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// When morning came,
// the tent was gone.
//
// In its place
// stood a well,
// brimming with clear water.
//
// Beside it,
// a young boy
// knelt in the sand,
// rubbing a shard of brass
// no larger than the palm of his hand.
//
// Zahir said nothing.
//
// There are moments
// when words
// only make wisdom smaller.
//
// He unwound
// his old turban,
// placed it gently
// in the boy's hands,
// and whispered:
//
// "When it speaks,
// do not ask for treasure.
//
// Do not ask for kingdoms.
//
// Do not ask
// for a longer life.
//
// Ask it instead
// the one question
// it fears answering
// more than any other.
//
// That
// is where
// the lamp
// truly begins
// to shine."
//
// The boy nodded.
//
// He rubbed
// the brass.
//
// The desert
// held its breath.
//
// For the first time
// in a thousand nights,
//
// the wind
// brought
// no secrets.
//
// It brought
// space.
//
// A silence
// wide enough
// for a new question
// to be born.
//
// For the greatest magic
// of the genies
// has never been
// the granting of wishes.
//
// Their greatest magic
// is reminding us
// that we ourselves
// are the lamp.
//
// We are
// the oil.
//
// The flame.
//
// The hand
// that kindles it.
//
// And the night
// through which
// its light
// must travel.
//
// All at once.
//
// Such
// is the condition
// of being human:
//
// To keep
// rubbing
// the bronze
// of the world
// until it answers
// in your own voice.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A Moral That Refuses to Moralize,
// for Morality Itself Is Just Another Wall
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Whoever seeks
// the genie
// outside himself
// finds only
// an echo.
//
// Whoever accepts
// becoming
// the question
// becomes
// the storm.
//
// And every storm,
// sooner or later,
//
// learns
// that the deepest rain
// is the one
// that falls
// inward.
//
// = End =
//
//
